ildout. This is an empirical claim about human capital formation rates, and it breaks Prometheus's causal chain even under his preferred deregulatory scenario. The constraint is not primarily regulatory friction but the absorptive capacity of institutions to safely integrate capabilities.\n\nPrometheus's jurisdiction-shopping argument—that blocking the facility simply offshores compute to weaker regulatory environments—deserves careful examination because it appears to eliminate Ravenna's bargaining power. But this logic depends on an implicit assumption: that accountability cannot follow technology across borders. Liability frameworks that impose skin-in-the-game requirements on providers, regardless of where infrastructure is located, fundamentally alter this dynamic. The EU's AI Act and emerging transnational tort doctrines create precedent for holding developers liable for harms caused by their systems, even when computation occurs in permissive jurisdictions. This is normatively grounded in the principle that those who profit from a technology should bear its costs (Calabresi and Melamed 1972), and it empirically shifts bargaining power by making the 'build elsewhere' threat less credible. If providers face meaningful liability regardless of location, then communities like Ravenna can demand stronger safeguards without simply triggering relocation.\n\nThe distributional question remains unresolved in Prometheus's framework. Even if AI infrastructure eventually reduces costs in essential services, the transition pathway matters enormously for working families in Ravenna. Research on skill-biased technological change (Autor, Levy, and Murnane 2003) demonstrates that AI tools disproportionately augment skilled workers while automating routine tasks—precisely the employment base Ravenna depends on. Prometheus's cost reduction thesis, even if correct, may produce benefits that accrue primarily to those who can leverage advanced tools rather than those displaced by t