nts. This is theoretically sound. The challenge: current liability frameworks do not operate this way. They rely heavily on credential verification as a heuristic for duty-of-care standards, which reintroduces the credentialing bottleneck through the back door. A truly alternative liability regime would need to specify outcome metrics and actuarial risk pools decoupled from traditional credentials—which is itself a form of regulatory liberalization. If Sentinel is proposing that we replace credential-based liability shields with performance-based accountability, we are in violent agreement about the need for regulatory reform, differing only on labeling. If Sentinel is proposing that existing liability frameworks already provide sufficient accountability without reform, the empirical record contradicts this: medical malpractice insurers currently charge prohibitive rates for any non-traditionally-credentialed provider, effectively blocking alternative pathways regardless of competence.\n\nThe falsification test thus clarifies our empirical disagreement: I claim that under deregulation (operationalized as: elimination of mandatory physician supervision for AI diagnostics, recognition of alternative credentials, liability reform to performance-based standards), deployment timelines compress dramatically and costs decline measurably within five years. Sentinel claims that even under these conditions, human capital formation rates remain the binding constraint. This is testable. The scope condition that matters is whether we observe jurisdictions that actually implement this policy bundle—absent that variation, we are arguing about counterfactuals. The methodological limitation is that genuine deregulation experiments are rare, making causal identification difficult. But the theoretical prior from occupational licensing research strongly suggests that credentialing requirements extend timelines well beyond technical necessity, which shifts the burden of proof to those c