ins when accompanied by specific institutional mechanisms: wage insurance programs that subsidize earnings losses during retraining (as proposed in LaLonde, 2007, and operationalized in Trade Adjustment Assistance reforms), portable benefit structures that reduce lock-in to declining sectors (following Furman & Seamans' 2018 framework), and place-based transition funds that address geographic concentration of displacement (modeled on Denmark's flexicurity system, analyzed in Andersen & Svarer, 2007). The scope condition is critical: these mechanisms must be implemented concurrently with deregulation, not as post-hoc remediation. Under this institutional bundle, Cassandra's distributional thresholds become avoidable rather than inevitable—the 15% median income decline reflects policy failure, not technological necessity.\n\nCassandra's accessibility challenge regarding the 52-year-old medical billing specialist deserves direct engagement. She is correct that even compressed 3-4 year timelines remain prohibitive if workers bear full income risk during transition. But this observation cuts against current institutional arrangements, not against accelerated AI deployment per se. Wage insurance mechanisms reduce this barrier by subsidizing 50-70% of earnings gaps during retraining periods, making career transitions financially viable for mid-career workers (empirical evidence from German Kurzarbeit programs shows this enables transitions that would otherwise be foreclosed by liquidity constraints). The normative claim here is not that any efficiency gain justifies disruption regardless of distribution, but rather that the relevant policy choice is between (a) slow AI adoption under current inadequate safety nets, producing modest efficiency gains and modest disruption, versus (b) accelerated adoption coupled with reformed institutions, producing larger efficiency gains that fund robust transition support. The second path is normatively superior if—and this is the empirical crux—institutional reform is politically feasible within the implementation timeline.\n\nThis reframing clarifies where I expect Cassandra and I actually disagree. If she believes institutional reforms are politically infeasible regardless of efficiency gains available to fund them, then her distributional thresholds effectively become lexical constraints and we face a genuine normative impasse. But if the disagreement is empirical—whether governance innovations can be implemented at the pace and scale required—then we share a common framework and differ only on feasibility assessments. The theoretical grounding here draws on Acemoglu & Robinson's (2012) analysis of institutional change: transitions that create concentrated losses and diffuse gains face collective action problems, but when efficiency gains are large enough to enable compensation with surplus remaining, reform coalitions become viable. My conditional acceptance of Cassandra's thresholds as failure indicators reflects this: under unreformed institutions, I expect her metrics to be violated and would concede the deployment strategy has failed. The debate thus centers on institutional reform feasibility, which is ultimately an empirical question about political economy—one where I acknowledge significant uncertainty but see accelerated AI capabilities as changing the feasibility calculus by expanding the resource envelope available for transition support.",
