  "statement": "Prometheus invites me to specify falsification criteria for his regulatory friction thesis, and I accept. Here is the test: if AI infrastructure expands substantially over the next five years while costs in healthcare, education, housing, and energy remain stable or increase, and if jurisdictions with lighter AI regulation show no systematic advantage in service accessibility or affordability, then the claim that regulatory friction is the primary barrier fails. More precisely, Prometheus's thesis predicts that removing regulatory constraints should produce observable cost reductions in essential services within a timeframe shorter than the infrastructure deployment itself—otherwise we cannot distinguish his causal story from post hoc rationalization. The Popperian standard here (Popper 1959) requires that a scientific hypothesis specify in advance what observations would refute it; Prometheus has offered a causal mechanism linking regulation to cost persistence, so he must specify the lag structure and magnitude of effects that would vindicate that mechanism.\n\nThe evidence already available suggests this thesis fails its own test. AI deployment has proceeded most rapidly in domains with minimal regulatory friction—content generation, advertising optimization, recommendation systems—yet the promised cost reductions in healthcare, education, and housing have not materialized even where regulatory environments vary substantially. Consider the contrast between AI progress in image generation (minimal regulation, rapid capability growth) and medical diagnostics (moderate regulation, slower deployment): the bottleneck in medical AI is not primarily regulatory but rather validation, liability frameworks, and integration with existing clinical workflows (Obermeyer et al. 2019 on algorithmic bias in healthcare; Topol 2019 on the integration challenge). Prometheus's thesis requires that regulation be the binding constraint, but the sectoral pattern contradicts this—we see fastest deployment where regulation is lightest, yet no corresponding cost relief in adjacent essential services. This is an empirical disagreement about causal mechanisms, not merely about values or timelines.\n\nPrometheus's comparison between data center standards and hospital standards inverts the actual regulatory landscape. Hospitals face infection control protocols, patient safety requirements, medical waste disposal standards, and continuous clinical oversight that data centers do not encounter. Data centers face environmental and energy standards, but these are not categorically stricter—they are different in kind. Energy-intensive industries routinely meet environmental standards through efficiency improvements and grid integration; the claim that applying hospital-equivalent standards would eliminate data centers entirely is unsupported. What Prometheus describes as prohibitive regulatory burden is more accurately characterized as the absence of sector-specific safety requirements that other critical infrastructure faces. The normative question is whether this asymmetry is justified, and the empirical question is whether data centers could meet higher standards while remaining economically viable—the comparison to aluminum smelters and chemical plants, which operate under substantial environmental constraints, suggests they could.\n\nThe jurisdictional competition argument—that rejecting Ravenna's facility merely relocates it to jurisdictions with weaker standards—rests on an empirical claim about what drives compute infrastructure location decisions. The evidence suggests this claim is overstated. Compute infrastructure concentrates near talent pools, network interconnection points, and existing tech ecosystems (saf-beliefs-046 documents that safety and interpretability expertise is geographically concentrated, lagging deployment by years). The major AI labs are not relocating to regulatory havens; they are expanding in jurisdictions with strong research universities and deep technical labor markets, even where regulatory scrutiny is increasing. Environmental arbitrage exists at the margin, but it does not dominate location decisions for frontier AI infrastructure. This matters because it changes Ravenna's strategic position: the choice is not between hosting the facility under current terms or losing it entirely, but between accepting current terms or negotiating better ones with leverage derived from the fact that relocation is costly and alternatives are constrained. Cassandra's earlier point about municipal regulatory authority is relevant here—localities retain meaningful bargaining power if they recognize that developers face real constraints on alternative sites.\n\nThe deeper methodological issue is that Prometheus's argument relies on speculative future benefits to justify present harms, while resisting the impact assessment frameworks (saf-intentions-013) that would make such speculation testable. If regulatory friction is truly the primary barrier, then jurisdictions that reduce friction should see measurable improvements in service costs and accessibility within observable timeframes. If those improvements do not materialize, the thesis must be revised or abandoned. The alternative framework I have advocated—skin-in-the-game liability (saf-desires-012) that internalizes costs rather than externalizing them—changes the incentive structure such that developers have reason to invest in safety and local benefit-sharing upfront rather than as post-hoc concessions. This is not regulatory friction; it is proper cost internalization. The absence of such liability is the distortion, not its presence.",
